// WHAT THIS MODULE IS FOR, AND WHY IT IS NOT A DOCUMENTATION FIX.
//
// DESIGN's Building & checks section states, as the fact the whole two-lane split turns on, that
// this repository's `passing CI` ruleset is active, carries no bypass actors, and names exactly one
// required status check -- `witnesses`. Until this module, that sentence was the ONLY place the
// fact lived on our side of the boundary. The setting itself was a hand-maintained web form: no
// authority declared it, nothing read it back, and nothing in the tree went red if it changed. The
// aggregation job in gunbc.witness_floor_workflow exists precisely because a required context is
// produced by a JOB and a second job's check is not required -- which means the safety of that
// whole design rests on a value nobody in this repository could observe.
//
// That is the specification-without-execution failure DESIGN section 5 names, at the outermost
// boundary the project has: a claim about the world, typed nowhere, checked by nothing. A prose
// sentence is not a consumer. If someone adds a second required context, removes the one that
// gates, flips enforcement to `evaluate`, or grants a bypass role, every gate inside this
// repository stays green while nothing it produces can block a merge any more.
//
// So this module is the ordinary converged-authority treatment, following gunbc.repo_local_git_config
// exactly: DESIRED state is declared, the live state is OBSERVED, the two are RECONCILED, drift is
// APPLIED, and the result is READ BACK and verified against desired again -- convergence is claimed
// only from the post-apply read, never from the apply's own success. The read-back is what makes it
// a converged authority rather than a fire-and-hope actuator, and it is the half a hand-run `gh api`
// command has never had.
//
// THE ONE FACT THAT STOPS BEING A NICKNAME. The required context's name is imported from
// gunbc.witness_floor_workflow witness_floor_workflow_job_id -- the same row the emitted workflow
// names its aggregation job with. Before this module those were two strings that happened to agree;
// now they are one fact read in two directions, so renaming the job cannot leave the ruleset naming
// a context nothing publishes (DESIGN section 3). That failure mode is specifically quiet: a
// required context that no job publishes leaves the check permanently pending rather than red.
// See the module-leading annotation. This row exists because the module's subject is a fact about
// the world rather than about the tree, and a reader arriving from gunbc.witness_floor_workflow
// needs the pointer.

// WHY THE RULESET IS ADDRESSED BY ID RATHER THAN BY NAME. GitHub's update endpoint is
// PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/rulesets/{ruleset_id}; the name is a mutable label and two rulesets may
// carry the same one. The id is the identity the API itself uses, so addressing by it is what makes
// "the ruleset we converge" and "the ruleset GitHub updates" the same subject rather than two
// lookups that happen to agree. The name is still declared and still verified -- a live ruleset at
// this id under a different name is drift this module reports, not a subject it silently accepts.
data gunbc_passing_ci_ruleset_id: Int = 16178731
data gunbc_passing_ci_ruleset_name: String = "passing CI"

// THE ONE REQUIRED CONTEXT, DERIVED. See the module annotation: this is the aggregation job's id,
// not a second spelling of it.
fn desired_required_contexts() -> List<String> {
  [witness_floor_workflow_job_id]
}

data desired_ruleset_enforcement: RulesetEnforcement = RulesetActive
data desired_ruleset_target: RulesetTarget = BranchTarget

// STRICT IS FALSE AND THAT IS A DECLARED POSITION, NOT AN OMISSION. A strict policy requires every
// branch to be up to date with the base before merging, which re-runs the ~30-minute floor for
// every pull request behind a merge. DESIGN's merge_group note records the merge queue as the
// construction that establishes the same invariant without that cost, and records that the queue is
// not configured yet. Declaring false here states the current position so that turning it on is a
// visible edit to this row rather than a web-form change nobody sees.
data desired_strict_required_status_checks_policy: Bool = false
data desired_do_not_enforce_on_create: Bool = false

data desired_ruleset_bypass_standing: ReaderBypassStanding = ThisReaderCannotBypass

// THE THREE RULES, IN THE ORDER GITHUB REPORTS THEM. deletion and non_fast_forward protect the
// branch's history; required_status_checks is the one that gates. They are declared together
// because the PUT is whole-ruleset (extdeps.github.rulesets Update): sending only the rule this
// module cares about would DELETE the other two, which is exactly the read-modify-write hazard a
// desired-state authority exists to remove.
// THE TYPE ROSTER IS DERIVED FROM THE RULES AND IS NO LONGER A SECOND LIST BESIDE THEM. It was
// authored as a literal `[DeletionRule, NonFastForwardRule, RequiredStatusChecksRule]` that a human
// kept in agreement with `desired_ruleset_rules` -- one fact with two authorities, agreeing by
// diligence. Anything reading the roster to reason about what converge SENDS was therefore reading
// a proxy that coincided with its subject rather than being derived from it, and a rule added to
// the projection without a matching hand-edit here would have been invisible to every such reader.
fn desired_ruleset_rule_types() -> List<RulesetRuleType> {
  ruleset_rule_types(rules: desired_ruleset_rules())
}

fn desired_required_status_checks() -> List<RequiredStatusCheckWire> {
  map(desired_required_contexts(), c =>
    required_status_check_row(context: c, integration_id: github_actions_app_integration_id))
}

// WHY NO merge_queue RULE IS PROJECTED UNDER EITHER ARM, STATED AS A STRUCTURAL BLOCKER RATHER
// THAN AS A TO-DO, because the difference decides whether the sign-off wall above is a guard or a
// placeholder. `RulesetRuleParametersWire` is the union of every rule's readable parameter shape,
// and two of its fields -- `strict_required_status_checks_policy` and `do_not_enforce_on_create` --
// are non-optional Bools belonging to required_status_checks. A merge_queue rule built through this
// record therefore cannot omit them, and sending them would put keys on the wire that GitHub does
// not accept on a merge_queue rule: the fabricated wire value the constructor above refuses in the
// other direction. The queue is not unprojected because someone forgot; it is unprojectable while
// the parameters record is one flat union.
//
// THE CAPABILITY THAT ENDS THIS, named as a capability rather than as an artifact: a wire decoding
// that admits a coproduct discriminated on a SIBLING KEY, so `parameters` can be per-rule-type and
// each rule carries exactly its own fields. The REST realization decodes nested objects into
// untagged maps today, which is why the union exists at all -- the same limitation this module's
// read side documents, observed here at the write end. Until that lands, every arm of the wall
// above is exercised against a fixture rule in `test.claim.repo_ruleset_witness_test`, and
// production refuses because production genuinely cannot express the rule.
fn desired_ruleset_rules() -> List<RulesetRuleWire> {
  [
    parameterless_rule(t: DeletionRule),
    parameterless_rule(t: NonFastForwardRule),
    required_status_checks_rule(
      checks: desired_required_status_checks(),
      strict: desired_strict_required_status_checks_policy,
      do_not_enforce_on_create: desired_do_not_enforce_on_create,
    ),
  ]
}

fn desired_ruleset_conditions() -> RulesetConditionsWire {
  ref_name_conditions(include: [default_branch_ref_alias], exclude: [])
}

// THE MERGE QUEUE IS DECLARED AS A PROPOSAL AND THE SIGN-OFF IS THE TYPE, NOT A COMMENT.
//
// The row above states that strict is false BECAUSE the merge queue is the construction that
// establishes the same invariant without re-running the floor for every pull request behind a
// merge, and that the queue is not configured yet. That sentence names a remedy no declaration
// carried, so "turn the queue on" was a web-form change nobody in this repository could see --
// exactly the condition this module exists to end for the required context.
//
// It is NOT declared as plain desired state, and the difference is the whole safety content.
// Desired state here is ACTUATED: converge PUTs the whole ruleset. Writing the queue into
// `desired_ruleset_rules` would mean the next converge run silently enables a merge queue on a
// live repository on the strength of one worker's edit. Enabling it changes how every author
// publishes, so it is an operator decision (DESIGN section 6: no scaffold, and no outward-facing
// change, lands by author declaration alone).
//
// So the desire is a coproduct with the sign-off IN IT. Under MergeQueueUnsigned the proposal is
// declared, readable, and diffable against the live ruleset, and contributes NO rule to the
// projection converge sends -- the settings are visible without being applied. Under
// MergeQueueSignedOff the operator's ruling is a required field, so the arm cannot be entered
// without recording who decided; that arm is what a later change flips, and flipping it is a
// one-row edit in this file rather than a web form.
// EVERY PARAMETER GITHUB REQUIRES, NOT A BOOLEAN. A `merge_queue_required: Bool` would say "queue
// on" while seven unmodeled parameters decided what the queue DOES -- provider defaults standing as
// a second authority beside our own declaration, which is the fork DESIGN section 3 forbids. So the
// policy is the complete documented parameter set of the merge_queue rule; there is no field left
// to a default.
type MergeQueuePolicy {
  grouping_strategy: MergeQueueGroupingStrategy
  merge_method: MergeQueueMergeMethod
  check_response_timeout: Minute
  max_entries_to_build: MergeQueueEntryCount
  max_entries_to_merge: MergeQueueEntryCount
  min_entries_to_merge: MergeQueueEntryCount
  min_entries_to_merge_wait: Minute
}

type MergeQueueDesire
  = MergeQueueUnsigned { proposal: MergeQueuePolicy, awaiting: DissolutionCondition }
  | MergeQueueSignedOff { policy: MergeQueuePolicy, ruling: NonEmptyStr }

// THE PROPOSED SETTINGS, EACH DERIVED RATHER THAN PICKED.
//
// grouping_strategy ALLGREEN: HEADGREEN admits an entry on the head entry's result alone, which
// re-opens the joint-failure class the queue is being proposed to close -- two changes each green
// against a base the other never existed on. ALLGREEN is the only strategy that makes the queue's
// guarantee the one this repository needs.
//
// merge_method SQUASH: the repository allows squash only (read live 2026-09-03: allow_squash_merge
// true, allow_merge_commit and allow_rebase_merge both false), and the queue merges as the app, so
// a queue configured to MERGE would compose merge commits past a repository setting that forbids
// them. SQUASH is what keeps the queue's publication the same shape as the one this repository
// already documents.
//
// check_response_timeout IS DERIVED FROM THE FLOOR'S OWN ENVELOPE AND IS NOT A CONSTANT HERE. It
// reads gunbc.witness_floor_workflow witness_floor_lane_timeout and adds a declared margin, both as Minute.
// A queue whose response timeout is BELOW the repository's execution envelope manufactures queue
// failures out of runs that were going to pass: it dequeues green work for being slow, and the
// author of the next timeout bump would have no way to know a second number tracked the first.
// Transcribing 180 here would have been exactly that second authority, so the number is read from
// the row that owns it -- and the witness asserts the ordering rather than the value, so raising
// the floor's timeout cannot silently invert it.
//
// THE ENTRY COUNTS ARE COMMISSIONING VALUES, NOT THROUGHPUT VALUES, and the difference is the
// whole reason they are 1. Merging one at a time means a failure names one pull request rather
// than a batch. Building one at a time means that at any moment ONE composition is in flight, so a
// failed commissioning run says which protocol step failed rather than which of five candidates
// was involved. A queue is adopted to make landings deterministic; starting it at its throughput
// setting rather than its legible one inverts that, and the throughput is recoverable later while
// a muddled first receipt is not.
//
// max_entries_to_build IS EXPECTED TO RISE, and it is a trigger rather than a note so it is
// retired by evidence and not by someone remembering. Building ahead of the head is what makes a
// candidate already validated when the head merges, which is real throughput and does not weaken
// ALLGREEN -- it is withheld only until the one-entry protocol is observed converging.
//
// min_entries_to_merge_wait 5 bounds how long a lone entry waits for a batch that is not coming,
// which is the starvation shape this proposal targets.
data merge_queue_build_concurrency_increase: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "DISSOLVE-ON: receipts from the commissioned queue show the ONE-ENTRY protocol converging -- entries composed, the required aggregate executing on each composition, and successful candidates advancing -- across enough landings to characterise a failure. max_entries_to_build then rises from 1 (5 is the intended value: it builds candidates ahead of the head so one is already validated when the head merges). Until those receipts exist, raising it puts several compositions in flight at once and a failure stops naming which protocol step produced it. This is a legibility bound on commissioning, not a claim that 1 is the right steady-state value.")
// The margin covers queue-side scheduling either side of the check itself -- the composition being
// built and the result being reported -- which is time the floor's own job timeout does not bound.
data merge_queue_response_margin: Minute = minute(count: 30)

fn merge_queue_check_response_timeout() -> Minute {
  measure_add(a: witness_floor_lane_timeout, b: merge_queue_response_margin)
}

data gunbc_merge_queue_proposal: MergeQueuePolicy = MergeQueuePolicy {
  grouping_strategy: AllGreenGrouping,
  merge_method: QueueSquash,
  check_response_timeout: merge_queue_check_response_timeout(),
  max_entries_to_build: merge_queue_entry_count(count: 1),
  max_entries_to_merge: merge_queue_entry_count(count: 1),
  min_entries_to_merge: merge_queue_entry_count(count: 1),
  min_entries_to_merge_wait: minute(count: 5),
}

// DO NOT ENABLE THE QUEUE IN THE GITHUB WEB UI. THE FLIP WOULD REVERSE ITSELF.
//
// This is sharper than the ordinary objection to out-of-band actuation, and it is stated at the
// sign-off row because that is where a future operator will be tempted to take the shortcut. This
// module emits the ruleset as a WHOLE-RULESET PUT of exactly the rules it declares. A merge_queue
// rule enabled through the web form is therefore unmodeled, and the next converge run would send a
// ruleset without it -- deleting it. A UI flip is not a change that skips the model; it is a change
// with a SCHEDULED, SILENT SELF-REVERSAL. (The actuation refusal below now stops that specific
// deletion from happening quietly, which turns the reversal into a stopped line -- but a stopped
// converge is not the queue being configured either. The route that works is signing this row.)
data desired_merge_queue: MergeQueueDesire = MergeQueueUnsigned {
  proposal: gunbc_merge_queue_proposal,
  awaiting: unbound_dissolution(description: "DISSOLVE-ON: the operator rules on enabling the merge queue for gunb-ai/gunbc. The ruling replaces this arm with MergeQueueSignedOff carrying the ruling text, at which point gunbc_merge_queue_proposal becomes actuated desired state and a live ruleset without the rule is a divergence converge repairs. Until then the proposal is declared and diffed and never written, and converge REFUSES to actuate at all while the live ruleset carries a merge_queue rule this projection cannot express -- see merge_queue_projection_refusal."),
}

fn desired_merge_queue_policy() -> MergeQueuePolicy? {
  match desired_merge_queue {
    MergeQueueUnsigned { proposal: _, awaiting: _ } => none
    MergeQueueSignedOff { policy: p, ruling: _ } => Present { value: p }
  }
}

// THE READ IS AN OBSERVATION WITH FOUR OUTCOMES, NOT A RULESET OR NOTHING. A ruleset that could not
// be read is not a ruleset with no rules, and the three ways a read fails do not share a remedy: a
// status refusal means a remote authority answered and its body usually says why; a transport
// refusal means nothing was answered at all, so nothing about the ruleset has been learned; an
// undecodable body means an answer arrived that this module could not read, which is a contract
// fault rather than an access one. Collapsing any of them into an empty observation would report
// "no required context" for "we could not look", and this module's whole verdict turns on that
// distinction -- the empty-observation narrow DESIGN names.
type RulesetReadRefusal
  = RulesetReadStatusRefused { status: HttpStatus, body: String }
  | RulesetReadTransportRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | RulesetReadBodyUndecodable { status: HttpStatus, cause: NonEmptyStr }

type RulesetObservation
  = RulesetObserved {
      name: String
      enforcement: RulesetEnforcement
      target: RulesetTarget
      bypass: ReaderBypassStanding
      include: List<String>
      exclude: List<String>
      rule_types: List<RulesetRuleType>
      standing: RequiredStatusCheckStanding
      merge_queue: MergeQueueStanding
      bypass_roster: BypassActorRosterObservation
    }
  | RulesetUnreadable { cause: RulesetReadRefusal }

fn read_refusal_of_outcome(outcome: RestOutcome) -> RulesetReadRefusal? {
  match outcome {
    RestOk => none
    RestStatusRefused { status: s, body: b } =>
      Present { value: RulesetReadStatusRefused { status: s, body: b } }
    RestTransportRefused { cause: c } =>
      Present { value: RulesetReadTransportRefused { cause: c } }
    RestBodyUndecodable { status: s, cause: c } =>
      Present { value: RulesetReadBodyUndecodable { status: s, cause: c } }
  }
}

fn render_read_refusal(cause: RulesetReadRefusal) -> String {
  match cause {
    RulesetReadStatusRefused { status: s, body: b } =>
      join(["ruleset read refused with status ", to_string(s), ": ", b], "")
    RulesetReadTransportRefused { cause: c } =>
      concat("ruleset read never reached GitHub: ", c)
    RulesetReadBodyUndecodable { status: s, cause: c } =>
      join(["ruleset read returned status ", to_string(s), " with an unreadable body: ", c], "")
  }
}

fn observe_repo_ruleset(owner: String, repo: String, ruleset_id: Int) -> RulesetObservation {
  let r = github.Rulesets.Get(
    auth_token: "",
    owner: owner,
    repo: repo,
    ruleset_id: ruleset_id,
  )
  match read_refusal_of_outcome(outcome: r.outcome) {
    Present { value: cause } => RulesetUnreadable { cause: cause }
    Absent => RulesetObserved {
      name: r.name,
      enforcement: parse_ruleset_enforcement(raw: r.enforcement),
      target: parse_ruleset_target(raw: r.target),
      bypass: parse_reader_bypass_standing(raw: r.current_user_can_bypass),
      include: r.conditions.ref_name.include,
      exclude: r.conditions.ref_name.exclude,
      rule_types: ruleset_rule_types(rules: r.rules),
      standing: required_status_check_standing(rules: r.rules),
      merge_queue: merge_queue_standing(rules: r.rules),
      bypass_roster: bypass_roster_unavailable_from_get(
        reader: "the credential this module's github.Rulesets.Get runs as"
      ),
    }
  }
}

// THE CONTEXT ROSTER IS RECONCILED, NOT COMPARED. gunbc.membership_reconcile is the repository's
// grain-agnostic desired-vs-observed diff and it answers the question this module actually has:
// which contexts are missing, which are present that should not be, and which cannot be acted on.
// Ownership is Owned rather than Ensured -- the opposite of gunbc.repo_local_git_config's choice,
// and deliberately so. There, a binding absent from desired is someone else's git config and is
// never torn down. Here, "witnesses is the ONE required context" is the whole claim: an extra
// required context is exactly the drift this module exists to see, so a removal must be PLANNED
// rather than refused. Ensured would have made the module blind to the additive half of its own
// subject.
type RequiredContextMemberAt = String

fn required_context_key(c: RequiredStatusCheckWire) -> String { c.context }
fn required_context_key_eq(a: String, b: String) -> Bool { a == b }
fn required_context_value_eq(a: RequiredStatusCheckWire, b: RequiredStatusCheckWire) -> Bool {
  a.integration_id == b.integration_id
}
fn required_context_ownership(_: RequiredStatusCheckWire) -> Ownership? {
  Present { value: Owned }
}

fn required_context_reconcile(
  observed: List<RequiredStatusCheckWire>,
) -> MembershipPlan<RequiredStatusCheckWire, RequiredContextMemberAt> {
  membership_plan_admitting_outcome(
    outcome: membership_reconcile(
      desired: desired_required_status_checks(),
      observed: observed,
      key_of: required_context_key,
      key_eq: required_context_key_eq,
      value_eq: required_context_value_eq,
      ownership_of: required_context_ownership,
    ),
  )
}

// EVERY WAY THE LIVE RULESET CAN DISAGREE WITH DESIRED, AS A VALUE. A Bool verdict here would be
// the collapse DESIGN keeps recording: "the ruleset is wrong" carries no remedy, and the remedies
// differ completely -- a missing context is fixed by an apply, a granted bypass role is fixed by a
// person, and an unrecognized enforcement string means GitHub's vocabulary moved and this module's
// reading is no longer established.
type RulesetDivergence
  = RulesetNameDrift { observed: String }
  | RulesetEnforcementDrift { observed: String }
  | RulesetTargetDrift { observed: String }
  | RulesetBypassGranted { observed: String }
  | ReaderBypassStandingUnrecognized { raw: String }
  | RefNameIncludeDrift { observed: List<String> }
  | RefNameExcludeNonEmpty { observed: List<String> }
  | RulesetRuleAbsent { rule: String }
  | RequiredStatusChecksRuleAbsent
  | RequiredStatusChecksRuleAmbiguous { count: Int }
  | RequiredContextMissing { context: String }
  | RequiredContextUnexpected { context: String }
  | RequiredContextUnactionable { count: Int }
  | StrictPolicyDrift { observed: Bool }
  | DoNotEnforceOnCreateDrift { observed: Bool }
  | RulesetRuleUnexpected { rule: String }
  | MergeQueueUndeclared { grouping_strategy: String, merge_method: String }
  | MergeQueueRuleAbsent
  | MergeQueueRuleAmbiguous { count: Int }
  | MergeQueueParametersUnreadable { missing: List<String> }
  | MergeQueueSettingDrift { setting: String, observed: String, desired: String }

fn enforcement_divergences(observed: RulesetEnforcement) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  let label = ruleset_enforcement_wire_label(e: observed)
  if label == ruleset_enforcement_wire_label(e: desired_ruleset_enforcement) {
    []
  } else {
    [RulesetEnforcementDrift { observed: label }]
  }
}

fn target_divergences(observed: RulesetTarget) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  let label = ruleset_target_wire_label(t: observed)
  if label == ruleset_target_wire_label(t: desired_ruleset_target) {
    []
  } else {
    [RulesetTargetDrift { observed: label }]
  }
}

// BYPASS IS READ THROUGH current_user_can_bypass AND THAT IS READER-RELATIVE, WHICH IS STATED
// RATHER THAN GLOSSED. GitHub answers this field for the identity that made the request, so a
// `never` here establishes that THIS reader cannot walk past the rule, not that no actor anywhere
// can. It is still the strongest signal the read returns -- an administrator token reading `never`
// means no bypass role covers an administrator -- and it is strictly better than the previous state
// of no observation at all. The honest bound: a bypass role granted to an actor this module never
// authenticates as would not be seen here, and closing that needs the bypass_actors roster, which
// GitHub omits from the response entirely when it is empty rather than sending [].
//
// AND THE NON-`never` ARMS ARE NAMED RATHER THAN WILDCARDED, which review 56496 caught this
// function getting wrong. A single `_` arm emitting one static sentence was total over the type
// and blind one level down -- exactly the pattern the rest of this module is written to avoid.
// Three facts sat under it with three different remedies: `pull_requests_only` and `always` are
// real, DIFFERENT bypass grants (revoke the role -- and which role differs), while an
// unrecognized standing means GitHub's vocabulary moved and this module's reading is no longer
// established, whose remedy is to extend the parser and re-establish the read. Collapsing the
// third into the first two would report a granted bypass for an unread one, and the `raw` GitHub
// actually sent -- the only thing that says what to extend the parser WITH -- was discarded.
fn bypass_divergences(observed: ReaderBypassStanding) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  match observed {
    ThisReaderCannotBypass => []
    ThisReaderCanBypassPullRequestsOnly =>
      [RulesetBypassGranted { observed: reader_bypass_standing_wire_label(b: observed) }]
    ThisReaderCanBypassAlways =>
      [RulesetBypassGranted { observed: reader_bypass_standing_wire_label(b: observed) }]
    UnrecognizedReaderBypassStanding { raw: r } => [ReaderBypassStandingUnrecognized { raw: r }]
  }
}

fn rule_presence_divergences(observed: List<RulesetRuleType>) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  fold(desired_ruleset_rule_types(), init: [], f: fn(acc, wanted) {
    let label = ruleset_rule_type_wire_label(t: wanted)
    if any(observed, o => ruleset_rule_type_wire_label(t: o) == label) {
      acc
    } else {
      append(acc, items: [RulesetRuleAbsent { rule: label }])
    }
  })
}

// THE UNEXPECTED DIRECTION, WHICH WAS MISSING AND IS NOT COSMETIC. The fold above answers "is
// every rule we declared present". Nothing answered "is every rule present one we declared", and
// the PUT is a WHOLE-RULESET REPLACE: a rule that exists live and is absent from
// desired_ruleset_rules is DELETED by the next converge run, with no divergence reported before or
// after, because the read-back compares against the same desired that dropped it. That is the
// read-modify-write hazard the module annotation says a desired-state authority exists to remove,
// left open in the one direction nobody had a live example of -- until the merge queue became a
// rule this repository might carry.
fn rule_unexpected_divergences(observed: List<RulesetRuleType>) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  fold(observed, init: [], f: fn(acc, got) {
    let label = ruleset_rule_type_wire_label(t: got)
    if any(desired_ruleset_rule_types(), w => ruleset_rule_type_wire_label(t: w) == label) {
      acc
    } else {
      append(acc, items: [RulesetRuleUnexpected { rule: label }])
    }
  })
}

// A LIVE RULE THIS PROJECTION CANNOT EXPRESS IS AN ACTUATION REFUSAL, NOT A DIVERGENCE TO REPAIR.
// A divergence means "the world is not what we declared, apply desired". For an unexpected rule
// that reading is exactly backwards: applying desired is what DESTROYS it. So converge stops the
// line here rather than repairing anything -- the DESIGN section 5 factory model, where a deficit
// halts the run and analysis precedes restart. `verify` still reports it as a divergence, because
// reporting is what verify is for.
// A SIGNED DESIRE THIS PROJECTION CANNOT BUILD IS ALSO AN ACTUATION REFUSAL, and it is here rather
// than in a follow-up note because the trap is otherwise latent. desired_ruleset_rules returns the
// three declared rules and appends NO merge_queue rule under either arm -- wiring that projection
// is the increment after sign-off, because the queue rule must send its own parameter keys without
// the required_status_checks keys beside it. So the day the arm flips to MergeQueueSignedOff and
// nothing else changes, converge would PUT a ruleset with no queue in it while verify reported a
// MergeQueueRuleAbsent divergence no apply could ever clear: a signed policy silently not in
// force, which is the worst of the available states.
//
// Refusing instead makes the flip fail LOUDLY the moment it happens, naming what is missing --
// which turns "do not forget the follow-up" from a comment somebody must read into a line-stop the
// sign-off itself trips (DESIGN section 5: the deficit stops the line, and analysis precedes
// restart).
//
// IT TAKES THE POLICY AS AN OPERAND SO ITS RED IS AUTHORABLE. The signed state does not exist in
// this tree -- the desire row is Unsigned -- so a check reading only that row would be permanently
// green by construction and would be cited as coverage while asserting nothing (DESIGN section
// 4b: ask whether the RED can be written before writing the check). A witness can hand it the
// proposal as though signed, and does.
// THE WALL READS THE PROJECTED RULES THEMSELVES, NOT A ROSTER THAT TRACKS THEM.
//
// Its first form asked whether `desired_ruleset_rule_types()` contained a merge_queue entry. That
// question was answerable without ever touching what converge would send: the roster was a second
// hand-maintained list, so the check passed or failed on the agreement between two authorities
// rather than on the projection. Even with the roster now derived, a rule TYPE says nothing about
// the seven parameters that decide what the queue actually does -- a merge_queue rule projected
// with the wrong strategy, or with a parameter missing, carries the right type and the wrong queue.
//
// So the subject is the rule list, and it is read with `merge_queue_standing` -- THE SAME READER
// THAT INTERPRETS THE LIVE GET. That is DESIGN section 4's one-grammar-both-directions applied to
// this module: the projection is judged by the identical procedure that judges the live ruleset, so
// "the signed policy is in force" and "the signed policy will be sent" cannot drift into two
// different questions with two different answers. Every arm of the reader becomes a located
// refusal, and per-field drift is reported as the field, the declared value and the projected one.
type SignedProjectionRefusal
  = NoQueueRuleProjected
  | ProjectedQueueParametersIncomplete { missing: List<String> }
  | ProjectedQueueAmbiguous { count: Int }
  | ProjectedQueueSettingDrift { field: String, declared: String, projected: String }

fn projected_queue_drift(policy: MergeQueuePolicy, standing: MergeQueueStanding) -> List<SignedProjectionRefusal> {
  match standing {
    NoMergeQueueRule => [NoQueueRuleProjected]
    MultipleMergeQueueRules { count: c } => [ProjectedQueueAmbiguous { count: c }]
    MergeQueueParametersIncomplete { missing: m } => [ProjectedQueueParametersIncomplete { missing: m }]
    MergeQueueConfigured {
      grouping_strategy: g,
      merge_method: mm,
      check_response_timeout: t,
      max_entries_to_build: b,
      max_entries_to_merge: x,
      min_entries_to_merge: n,
      min_entries_to_merge_wait: w,
    } => concat_lists([
      drift_str(
        field: "grouping_strategy",
        declared: merge_queue_grouping_strategy_wire_label(g: policy.grouping_strategy),
        projected: merge_queue_grouping_strategy_wire_label(g: g),
      ),
      drift_str(
        field: "merge_method",
        declared: merge_queue_merge_method_wire_label(m: policy.merge_method),
        projected: merge_queue_merge_method_wire_label(m: mm),
      ),
      drift_minute(field: "check_response_timeout_minutes", declared: policy.check_response_timeout, projected: t),
      drift_entries(field: "max_entries_to_build", declared: policy.max_entries_to_build, projected: b),
      drift_entries(field: "max_entries_to_merge", declared: policy.max_entries_to_merge, projected: x),
      drift_entries(field: "min_entries_to_merge", declared: policy.min_entries_to_merge, projected: n),
      drift_minute(field: "min_entries_to_merge_wait_minutes", declared: policy.min_entries_to_merge_wait, projected: w),
    ])
  }
}

fn concat_lists(xs: List<List<SignedProjectionRefusal>>) -> List<SignedProjectionRefusal> {
  fold(xs, init: [], f: fn(acc, more) { append(acc, items: more) })
}

fn drift_str(field: String, declared: String, projected: String) -> List<SignedProjectionRefusal> {
  if declared == projected {
    []
  } else {
    [ProjectedQueueSettingDrift { field: field, declared: declared, projected: projected }]
  }
}

fn drift_minute(field: String, declared: Minute, projected: Minute) -> List<SignedProjectionRefusal> {
  drift_str(
    field: field,
    declared: to_string(minute_count(declared)),
    projected: to_string(minute_count(projected)),
  )
}

fn drift_entries(field: String, declared: MergeQueueEntryCount, projected: MergeQueueEntryCount) -> List<SignedProjectionRefusal> {
  drift_str(
    field: field,
    declared: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(declared)),
    projected: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(projected)),
  )
}

fn render_signed_projection_refusal(r: SignedProjectionRefusal) -> String {
  match r {
    NoQueueRuleProjected => "no merge_queue rule is projected at all, so applying desired would PUT a ruleset without the signed queue and the divergence would never clear"
    ProjectedQueueAmbiguous { count: c } => concat("the projection carries more than one merge_queue rule, count ", to_string(c))
    ProjectedQueueParametersIncomplete { missing: m } => concat("the projected merge_queue rule is missing parameters: ", join(m, ", "))
    ProjectedQueueSettingDrift { field: f, declared: d, projected: p } =>
      concat(concat(concat(concat(concat("projected ", f), " is "), p), " but the signed policy declares "), d)
  }
}

fn signed_desire_projection_refusal_for(
  policy: MergeQueuePolicy?,
  rules: List<RulesetRuleWire>,
) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match policy {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: p } => {
      let drift = projected_queue_drift(policy: p, standing: merge_queue_standing(rules: rules))
      if length(drift) == 0 {
        none
      } else {
        Present { value: concat(
          "the merge queue is signed off but the desired projection does not carry the signed policy, so converge would send a ruleset that cannot converge. Refusing rather than actuating. ",
          join(map(drift, d => render_signed_projection_refusal(r: d)), "; "),
        ) }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn signed_desire_projection_refusal() -> NonEmptyStr? {
  signed_desire_projection_refusal_for(policy: desired_merge_queue_policy(), rules: desired_ruleset_rules())
}

fn merge_queue_projection_refusal(rule_types: List<RulesetRuleType>) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  let unexpected = rule_unexpected_divergences(observed: rule_types)
  if length(unexpected) == 0 {
    signed_desire_projection_refusal()
  } else {
    Present { value: concat(
      "the live ruleset carries rules this repository's desired projection does not declare, and converge sends the WHOLE ruleset: applying desired would delete them. Refusing rather than repairing. ",
      render_divergences(ds: unexpected),
    ) }
  }
}

fn ref_name_divergences(include: List<String>, exclude: List<String>) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  let want = [default_branch_ref_alias]
  let include_ok = length(include) == length(want)
    && all(want, w => any(include, i => i == w))
  let head = if include_ok { [] } else { [RefNameIncludeDrift { observed: include }] }
  if length(exclude) == 0 {
    head
  } else {
    append(head, items: [RefNameExcludeNonEmpty { observed: exclude }])
  }
}

fn context_roster_divergences(
  contexts: List<String>,
  strict: Bool,
  do_not_enforce_on_create: Bool,
) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  let missing = fold(desired_required_contexts(), init: [], f: fn(acc, want) {
    if any(contexts, c => c == want) {
      acc
    } else {
      append(acc, items: [RequiredContextMissing { context: want }])
    }
  })
  let unexpected = fold(contexts, init: missing, f: fn(acc, got) {
    if any(desired_required_contexts(), w => w == got) {
      acc
    } else {
      append(acc, items: [RequiredContextUnexpected { context: got }])
    }
  })
  let with_strict = if strict == desired_strict_required_status_checks_policy {
    unexpected
  } else {
    append(unexpected, items: [StrictPolicyDrift { observed: strict }])
  }
  if do_not_enforce_on_create == desired_do_not_enforce_on_create {
    with_strict
  } else {
    append(with_strict, items: [DoNotEnforceOnCreateDrift { observed: do_not_enforce_on_create }])
  }
}

fn standing_divergences(standing: RequiredStatusCheckStanding) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  match standing {
    NoRequiredStatusChecksRule => [RequiredStatusChecksRuleAbsent]
    MultipleRequiredStatusChecksRules { count: n } =>
      [RequiredStatusChecksRuleAmbiguous { count: n }]
    RequiredStatusChecks { contexts: c, strict: s, do_not_enforce_on_create: d } =>
      context_roster_divergences(contexts: c, strict: s, do_not_enforce_on_create: d)
  }
}

fn setting_drift(setting: String, observed: String, desired: String) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  if observed == desired {
    []
  } else {
    [MergeQueueSettingDrift { setting: setting, observed: observed, desired: desired }]
  }
}

fn merge_queue_setting_drifts(
  policy: MergeQueuePolicy,
  grouping: MergeQueueGroupingStrategy,
  method: MergeQueueMergeMethod,
  timeout: Minute,
  build: MergeQueueEntryCount,
  merge_max: MergeQueueEntryCount,
  merge_min: MergeQueueEntryCount,
  wait: Minute,
) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  fold(
    [
      setting_drift(
        setting: "merge_method",
        observed: merge_queue_merge_method_wire_label(m: method),
        desired: merge_queue_merge_method_wire_label(m: policy.merge_method),
      ),
      setting_drift(
        setting: "check_response_timeout_minutes",
        observed: to_string(minute_count(timeout)),
        desired: to_string(minute_count(policy.check_response_timeout)),
      ),
      setting_drift(
        setting: "max_entries_to_build",
        observed: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(build)),
        desired: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(policy.max_entries_to_build)),
      ),
      setting_drift(
        setting: "max_entries_to_merge",
        observed: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(merge_max)),
        desired: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(policy.max_entries_to_merge)),
      ),
      setting_drift(
        setting: "min_entries_to_merge",
        observed: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(merge_min)),
        desired: to_string(merge_queue_entry_count_value(policy.min_entries_to_merge)),
      ),
      setting_drift(
        setting: "min_entries_to_merge_wait_minutes",
        observed: to_string(minute_count(wait)),
        desired: to_string(minute_count(policy.min_entries_to_merge_wait)),
      ),
    ],
    init: setting_drift(
      setting: "grouping_strategy",
      observed: merge_queue_grouping_strategy_wire_label(g: grouping),
      desired: merge_queue_grouping_strategy_wire_label(g: policy.grouping_strategy),
    ),
    f: fn(acc, more) { append(acc, items: more) },
  )
}

// THE UNSIGNED ARM IS NOT SILENT ABOUT A LIVE QUEUE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF DECLARING A PROPOSAL
// AT ALL. Unsigned + absent is the state this repository is in and is no divergence. Unsigned +
// PRESENT means somebody enabled a merge queue through the web form -- the exact change this
// module exists to stop being invisible -- so it is reported, with the two fields that decide what
// guarantee the live queue actually provides. It is deliberately NOT repaired: the remedy for an
// undeclared queue is a ruling, not a delete.
fn merge_queue_divergences(observed: MergeQueueStanding) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  match observed {
    MultipleMergeQueueRules { count: n } => [MergeQueueRuleAmbiguous { count: n }]
    MergeQueueParametersIncomplete { missing: ms } => [MergeQueueParametersUnreadable { missing: ms }]
    NoMergeQueueRule =>
      match desired_merge_queue_policy() {
        Absent => []
        Present { value: _ } => [MergeQueueRuleAbsent]
      }
    MergeQueueConfigured {
      grouping_strategy: g,
      merge_method: m,
      check_response_timeout: t,
      max_entries_to_build: b,
      max_entries_to_merge: xm,
      min_entries_to_merge: nm,
      min_entries_to_merge_wait: w,
    } =>
      match desired_merge_queue_policy() {
        Absent => [MergeQueueUndeclared {
          grouping_strategy: merge_queue_grouping_strategy_wire_label(g: g),
          merge_method: merge_queue_merge_method_wire_label(m: m),
        }]
        Present { value: p } => merge_queue_setting_drifts(
          policy: p,
          grouping: g,
          method: m,
          timeout: t,
          build: b,
          merge_max: xm,
          merge_min: nm,
          wait: w,
        )
      }
  }
}

fn name_divergences(observed: String) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  if observed == gunbc_passing_ci_ruleset_name {
    []
  } else {
    [RulesetNameDrift { observed: observed }]
  }
}

fn observed_ruleset_divergences(
  name: String,
  enforcement: RulesetEnforcement,
  target: RulesetTarget,
  bypass: ReaderBypassStanding,
  include: List<String>,
  exclude: List<String>,
  rule_types: List<RulesetRuleType>,
  standing: RequiredStatusCheckStanding,
  merge_queue: MergeQueueStanding,
) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  fold(
    [
      enforcement_divergences(observed: enforcement),
      target_divergences(observed: target),
      bypass_divergences(observed: bypass),
      ref_name_divergences(include: include, exclude: exclude),
      rule_presence_divergences(observed: rule_types),
      rule_unexpected_divergences(observed: rule_types),
      standing_divergences(standing: standing),
      merge_queue_divergences(observed: merge_queue),
    ],
    init: name_divergences(observed: name),
    f: fn(acc, more) { append(acc, items: more) },
  )
}

fn render_divergence(d: RulesetDivergence) -> String {
  match d {
    RulesetNameDrift { observed: o } => concat("ruleset name is ", o)
    RulesetEnforcementDrift { observed: o } => concat("enforcement is ", o)
    RulesetTargetDrift { observed: o } => concat("target is ", o)
    RulesetBypassGranted { observed: o } => concat("reader may bypass this ruleset: ", o)
    ReaderBypassStandingUnrecognized { raw: r } =>
      concat("current_user_can_bypass is unrecognized: ", r)
    RefNameIncludeDrift { observed: o } => concat("ref_name include is ", join(o, "|"))
    RefNameExcludeNonEmpty { observed: o } => concat("ref_name exclude is non-empty: ", join(o, "|"))
    RulesetRuleAbsent { rule: r } => concat("rule absent: ", r)
    RequiredStatusChecksRuleAbsent => "no required_status_checks rule"
    RequiredStatusChecksRuleAmbiguous { count: n } =>
      concat("more than one required_status_checks rule: ", to_string(n))
    RequiredContextMissing { context: c } => concat("required context missing: ", c)
    RequiredContextUnexpected { context: c } => concat("unexpected required context: ", c)
    RequiredContextUnactionable { count: n } =>
      concat("required-context reconcile refused members: ", to_string(n))
    StrictPolicyDrift { observed: o } =>
      concat("strict_required_status_checks_policy is ", to_string(o))
    DoNotEnforceOnCreateDrift { observed: o } =>
      concat("do_not_enforce_on_create is ", to_string(o))
    RulesetRuleUnexpected { rule: r } => concat("undeclared rule present: ", r)
    MergeQueueUndeclared { grouping_strategy: g, merge_method: m } =>
      join(["a merge queue is configured that no signed desire declares: grouping ", g, ", merge method ", m], "")
    MergeQueueRuleAbsent => "merge_queue rule absent while the merge queue is signed off"
    MergeQueueRuleAmbiguous { count: n } =>
      concat("more than one merge_queue rule: ", to_string(n))
    MergeQueueParametersUnreadable { missing: ms } =>
      concat("merge_queue rule present but its parameters did not carry: ", join(ms, ", "))
    MergeQueueSettingDrift { setting: st, observed: o, desired: d } =>
      join(["merge queue ", st, " is ", o, ", desired ", d], "")
  }
}

fn render_divergences(ds: List<RulesetDivergence>) -> String {
  join(map(ds, d => render_divergence(d: d)), "; ")
}

fn observation_divergences(observation: RulesetObservation) -> List<RulesetDivergence> {
  match observation {
    RulesetUnreadable { cause: _ } => []
    RulesetObserved {
      name: n,
      enforcement: e,
      target: t,
      bypass: b,
      include: inc,
      exclude: exc,
      rule_types: rt,
      standing: st,
      merge_queue: mq,
      bypass_roster: _,
    } => observed_ruleset_divergences(
      name: n,
      enforcement: e,
      target: t,
      bypass: b,
      include: inc,
      exclude: exc,
      rule_types: rt,
      standing: st,
      merge_queue: mq,
    )
  }
}

// THE RECONCILE PLAN IS COMPUTED FROM THE OBSERVED CONTEXT ROSTER AND IS WHAT DECIDES WHETHER TO
// WRITE. It is not a second opinion beside the divergence list: the divergences answer "is the live
// ruleset what we declared", across every field; the plan answers "does the context roster need an
// apply", which is the only part of the subject this module can actuate. A bypass role is a real
// divergence with no apply.
fn observed_required_checks(standing: RequiredStatusCheckStanding) -> List<RequiredStatusCheckWire> {
  match standing {
    NoRequiredStatusChecksRule => []
    MultipleRequiredStatusChecksRules { count: _ } => []
    RequiredStatusChecks { contexts: c, strict: _, do_not_enforce_on_create: _ } =>
      map(c, ctx => required_status_check_row(
        context: ctx,
        integration_id: github_actions_app_integration_id,
      ))
  }
}

type RepoRulesetConvergedEvidence {
  writes_applied: Int
  removals_applied: Int
  required_contexts: List<String>
  enforcement: String
  reader_cannot_bypass: Bool
}

fn apply_desired_ruleset(owner: String, repo: String, ruleset_id: Int) -> RulesetReadRefusal? {
  let r = github.Rulesets.Update(
    auth_token: "",
    owner: owner,
    repo: repo,
    ruleset_id: ruleset_id,
    name: gunbc_passing_ci_ruleset_name,
    target: ruleset_target_wire_label(t: desired_ruleset_target),
    enforcement: ruleset_enforcement_wire_label(e: desired_ruleset_enforcement),
    conditions: desired_ruleset_conditions(),
    rules: desired_ruleset_rules(),
  )
  read_refusal_of_outcome(outcome: r.outcome)
}

fn converged_evidence_of(
  writes: Int,
  removals: Int,
  observation: RulesetObservation,
) -> RepoRulesetConvergedEvidence {
  match observation {
    RulesetUnreadable { cause: _ } => RepoRulesetConvergedEvidence {
      writes_applied: writes,
      removals_applied: removals,
      required_contexts: [],
      enforcement: "",
      reader_cannot_bypass: false,
    }
    RulesetObserved {
      name: _,
      enforcement: e,
      target: _,
      bypass: b,
      include: _,
      exclude: _,
      rule_types: _,
      standing: st,
      merge_queue: _,
      bypass_roster: _,
    } => RepoRulesetConvergedEvidence {
      writes_applied: writes,
      removals_applied: removals,
      required_contexts: map(observed_required_checks(standing: st), c => c.context),
      enforcement: ruleset_enforcement_wire_label(e: e),
      reader_cannot_bypass: match b { ThisReaderCannotBypass => true _ => false },
    }
  }
}

// THE CONVERGENCE CLAIM COMES FROM THE POST-APPLY READ AND FROM NOTHING ELSE. A 200 on the PUT says
// GitHub accepted a request; it does not say the ruleset now denies a merge that lacks the required
// context. So the apply's own success is never the evidence -- the second observation is, and it is
// judged by the same divergence fold the pre-apply read went through. That is the whole difference
// between this module and the `gh api` command it replaces.
fn repo_ruleset_converge_actuate_at(
  owner: String,
  repo: String,
  ruleset_id: Int,
) -> Reconciliation<RulesetObservation, RepoRulesetConvergedEvidence> {
  let before = observe_repo_ruleset(owner: owner, repo: repo, ruleset_id: ruleset_id)
  match before {
    RulesetUnreadable { cause: c } =>
      NotConverged { reason: render_read_refusal(cause: c), applied: before }
    RulesetObserved {
      name: _,
      enforcement: _,
      target: _,
      bypass: _,
      include: _,
      exclude: _,
      rule_types: rt,
      standing: st,
      merge_queue: _,
      bypass_roster: _,
    } => {
      let plan = required_context_reconcile(observed: observed_required_checks(standing: st))
      let refusals = membership_refusal_count(plan: plan)
      match merge_queue_projection_refusal(rule_types: rt) {
        Present { value: cause } => NotConverged { reason: cause, applied: before }
        Absent =>
      if refusals > 0 {
        NotConverged {
          reason: render_divergence(d: RequiredContextUnactionable { count: refusals }),
          applied: before,
        }
      } else {
        let writes = membership_write_count(plan: plan)
        let removals = membership_removal_count(plan: plan)
        let divergences_before = observation_divergences(observation: before)
        if length(divergences_before) == 0 {
          Converged {
            evidence: converged_evidence_of(writes: 0, removals: 0, observation: before),
            applied: before,
          }
        } else {
          match apply_desired_ruleset(owner: owner, repo: repo, ruleset_id: ruleset_id) {
            Present { value: c } =>
              NotConverged {
                reason: concat("ruleset apply refused: ", render_read_refusal(cause: c)),
                applied: before,
              }
            Absent => {
              let after = observe_repo_ruleset(owner: owner, repo: repo, ruleset_id: ruleset_id)
              match after {
                RulesetUnreadable { cause: rc } =>
                  NotConverged {
                    reason: concat("ruleset read-back after apply: ", render_read_refusal(cause: rc)),
                    applied: after,
                  }
                _ => {
                  let remaining = observation_divergences(observation: after)
                  if length(remaining) == 0 {
                    Converged {
                      evidence: converged_evidence_of(
                        writes: writes,
                        removals: removals,
                        observation: after,
                      ),
                      applied: after,
                    }
                  } else {
                    NotConverged {
                      reason: concat(
                        "ruleset read-back after apply still diverges: ",
                        render_divergences(ds: remaining),
                      ),
                      applied: after,
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn repo_ruleset_verify_at(
  owner: String,
  repo: String,
  ruleset_id: Int,
) -> Reconciliation<RulesetObservation, RepoRulesetConvergedEvidence> {
  let observed = observe_repo_ruleset(owner: owner, repo: repo, ruleset_id: ruleset_id)
  match observed {
    RulesetUnreadable { cause: c } =>
      NotConverged { reason: render_read_refusal(cause: c), applied: observed }
    _ => {
      let divergences = observation_divergences(observation: observed)
      if length(divergences) == 0 {
        Converged {
          evidence: converged_evidence_of(writes: 0, removals: 0, observation: observed),
          applied: observed,
        }
      } else {
        NotConverged { reason: render_divergences(ds: divergences), applied: observed }
      }
    }
  }
}

data repo_ruleset_converge_entry: String = "dag/gunbc/repo/repo_ruleset.dag"
data repo_ruleset_converge_function: String = "converge"
data repo_ruleset_verify_function: String = "verify"

// VERIFY IS READ-ONLY AND CONVERGE WRITES, AND THEY ARE TWO ENTRY POINTS RATHER THAN ONE WITH A
// FLAG. A flag whose effect is "do not actuate" is a mode on one operation; two entry points make
// the read-only path unable to write by construction, which is what a verifier enrolled in an
// automated run needs. NEITHER IS ENROLLED IN CI BY THIS CHANGE, and that is stated so the module
// is not read as wider than it is: what lands here is the authority, the read-back and the typed
// verdict. Enrolling `verify` as a required phase is a separate change with its own operator
// agreement, and until it happens the wall is a route somebody runs, not a gate.
func verify() -> ProcessExit {
  match repo_ruleset_verify_at(
    owner: gunbc_repository.owner,
    repo: gunbc_repository.name,
    ruleset_id: gunbc_passing_ci_ruleset_id,
  ) {
    Converged { evidence: _, applied: _ } => ExitSuccess
    NotConverged { reason: r, applied: _ } =>
      exit_failure(reason: concat("repo ruleset verify refused: ", r))
  }
}

func converge() -> ProcessExit {
  match repo_ruleset_converge_actuate_at(
    owner: gunbc_repository.owner,
    repo: gunbc_repository.name,
    ruleset_id: gunbc_passing_ci_ruleset_id,
  ) {
    Converged { evidence: _, applied: _ } => ExitSuccess
    NotConverged { reason: r, applied: _ } =>
      exit_failure(reason: concat("repo ruleset converge refused: ", r))
  }
}
